  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Monday)\nIssued at 1152 PM CDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nIt's currently a muggy night across the Mid-South, with\ntemperatures ranging from the upper 70s to mid-80s and heat\nindices holding steady near 90 degrees.\n\nAttention is focused on newly-formed Tropical Storm Bertha in the\nGulf. This system helped relax heights across the region on\nMonday, allowing for enough convective coverage to provide some\nheat relief. We're tracking Bertha on a nearly westerly path \nnear the Gulf Coast over the next 24 hours, which may impact\nstorm development across North Mississippi on Tuesday afternoon.\n\nMeanwhile, prepare for our most dangerous and oppressive heat of\nthis event on Tuesday. NBM 50th percentile guidance indicates\nwidespread, dangerous heat indices reaching up to 116\u00c2\u00b0F. The \nExtreme Heat Warning has been expanded eastward to include the\nmajority of west Tennessee and portions of northwest Mississippi.\nThe peak and cumulative effect of this current heatwave will\ncreate dangerous health risks for our most vulnerable-check on\nyour neighbors, stay hydrated, and avoid being outdoors between\n11 AM and 7 PM.\n\nA cold front will begin tracking south into the area late Tuesday\nafternoon. With an unstable airmass-MLCAPE values exceeding\n3000 J/kg-and bulk shear around 30 knots, organized severe storms\nare highly likely. While HREF guidance suggests we'll be in the\nright exit region of the mid-level jet (typically inhibiting\nlarge-scale lift), the combination of extreme instability and the\nincoming boundary should trigger strong to severe convection.\nStorm mode will favor multi-cells that could grow upscale into\nline clusters capable of producing severe wind gusts.\n\nThe current Slight Risk covers northeastern counties near the\nTennessee River, with a Marginal Risk across much of West\nTennessee, the Missouri Bootheel, and parts of Northeast\nArkansas. Based on the latest data, I anticipate the Slight Risk\nexpanding across West Tennessee and likely further west into\nNortheast Arkansas. Expect the primary threat to be damaging\nwind, supported by DCAPE values over 1000 J/kg, with the main\nwindow from late afternoon through midnight.\n\nThe front will lose momentum overnight as the parent trough lifts\nnortheast, then slowly traverse the region on Wednesday. Our\nsouthernmost counties may still see heat indices of 100-104\u00c2\u00b0F,\nbut heat products seem unlikely at this point. Significant relief\narrives Thursday; temperatures will feel \"downright cool\" by\ncomparison, with highs in the 80s and dewpoints dropping below\n70.\n\nThe late-week outlook hinges on the dissipation rate of TS Bertha\nas it tracks west. Current LREF and deterministic guidance\nsuggests dissipation by late Wednesday, which would allow the\nsubtropical ridge to nudge east, bringing back heat and humidity.\nHowever, if the system persists longer near the Gulf Coast, we'll\nlikely see a broad northwest flow, leading to near-normal highs\nin the low 90s and continued convective activity. Long-range\nguidance consistently shows the ridge amplifying across the\nPlains while troughing persists over the Ohio Valley, likely\npositioning us on the edge of the ridge under the main storm\ntrack.\n\nAC3",
